<Article title="4-Week Dead Hang Program: Build a Repeatable Baseline" description="A free 4-week dead hang planning framework with review points, editable timer settings and a local training log. No guaranteed results or paid signup.">
<div class="article-header"><div class="article-breadcrumb"><a href="/training-programs/">Training plans</a><span class="sep">/</span>4 weeks</div><h1 class="article-h1">4-week dead hang plan.</h1><p class="article-subtitle">Organise your sessions around a repeatable baseline. This is a flexible planning framework, not a promise to reach a particular hold time.</p></div>
<div class="article-body"><h2>Before the first session</h2><p>Use a securely fitted bar and a stable way to step down. Choose an assisted or unassisted hold you can control without pain. Keep that variation consistent when comparing results. If you have an injury or concerns about the movement, seek individual guidance.</p><h2>Your 4-week framework</h2>{blocks.map(([week,title,body])=><section class="plan-option"><p class="dh-eyebrow">{week}</p><h3>{title}</h3><p>{body}</p></section>)}<h2 id="session-structure">Plan two practice days</h2><p>Choose two non-consecutive days as an example schedule for this introductory block. Leave room to adjust around your other exercise and recovery. This schedule is an editorial starting framework rather than a tested dead-hang prescription.</p><ol><li>Check the bar and arrange a stable step. Use an easy supported hold to check that the setup feels comfortable.</li><li>Choose a short working interval that leaves you able to step down calmly. Enter that interval and your rest period in the timer.</li><li>Try the planned sets with the same assistance. End a set early if control slips. Rest longer or end the session if needed.</li><li>Record your best actual hold and the sets you completed. Write down the bar and support on the paper planner.</li></ol><p><strong>Example record:</strong> a visitor plans three 10-second supported holds with 60 seconds between holds. The actual holds are 10, 10 and 8 seconds. Their log entry is 10 seconds, feet supported, three sets. These numbers illustrate record keeping; they are not targets for every beginner.</p><h2>Choose your timer settings</h2><p>Enter your own hang duration, rest duration and number of sets. The workspace's defaults are examples. You can shorten a hold or increase rest before starting. The timer pauses when the page is hidden and never saves a session automatically.</p><a class="dh-btn" href="/training-log/">Open the timer & log</a><h2>Record the work you actually did</h2><p>Save the date, assistance, best actual hold and number of sets. Compare supported hangs with supported hangs and unassisted hangs with unassisted hangs. Export the log before clearing your browser or switching devices.</p><h2>When the plan needs changing</h2><p>Stop a painful hold. End a set before you lose the ability to step down under control. A missed session does not require making up extra sets. Repeating a block is an option; longer holds are not the only reason to keep a training record.</p><h2>Review the block</h2><p>Compare your current hold with an earlier hold made under the same conditions. The <a href="/dead-hang-calculator/">personal calculator</a> shows the difference without inventing a population percentile.</p><p>Use the <a href="/training-programs/#print-plan">printable session planner</a> if you prefer paper. Dead hangs are one part of a wider routine; the <a href="https://www.nhs.uk/live-well/exercise/physical-activity-guidelines-for-adults-aged-19-to-64/">NHS activity guidance</a> covers broader strength and aerobic activity.</p></div>
</Article>
